;; Layout conformance corpus: each case is rendered BOTH by cssom.layout and
;; by a real Blink browser (Brave; Chrome is the same engine), and the two
;; are compared on LINE STRUCTURE -- which text ends up on which line, in
;; which order -- rather than on exact pixel coordinates, because this
;; engine has no glyph shaping and its `(long (* 0.6 font-size))` character
;; approximation can never match a real font's metrics. Line structure is
;; the part that is genuinely comparable, and it is exactly the part this
;; engine has historically got wrong.
;;
;; Keep case content SHORT enough not to wrap at the harness width: a
;; wrapped run's break point DOES depend on real font metrics, so those
;; cases are reported separately (:wrapped) instead of being scored as
;; failures for a reason the comparison cannot legitimately judge.
;;
;; A failing case is a MEASUREMENT, not a bug report -- several here are
;; known, documented scope-cuts (floats, tables, <img>/form controls as
;; inline-level). They are in the corpus precisely so the coverage number
;; tells the truth about them.

